  "components": {
    "securitySchemes": {
      "bearerAuth": {
        "type": "http",
        "scheme": "bearer",
        "bearerFormat": "JWT",
        "x-semiont-limits": {
          "keySetMaxAgeSeconds": 600,
          "keyRefetchCooldownSeconds": 30,
          "keyFetchDeadlineSeconds": 5
        },
        "description": "`Authorization: Bearer <JWT>` — the scheme name in any case (RFC 7235); a header naming another scheme, or none, carries no credential, and nor does a cookie. The gateway accepts two kinds of token, told apart by `iss`.\n\n**Issuer tokens** — people and service accounts, minted by the knowledge base's trusted issuer (`[identity] issuer`). Signed RS256 by a key the issuer publishes at the `jwks_uri` its `/.well-known/openid-configuration` names. The gateway fetches that key set when it holds none, when the one it holds is `x-semiont-limits.keySetMaxAgeSeconds` old, and for a `kid` the set does not have once the set is `keyRefetchCooldownSeconds` old. A fetch is given `keyFetchDeadlineSeconds`, discovery included; one that fails is not tried again for `keyRefetchCooldownSeconds`, and requests that need the keys meanwhile share the one fetch or its failure. With no usable set, an issuer token is refused. `iss` must equal the configured issuer exactly. `aud` must contain this knowledge base's resource identifier: its did:web resolved to an https URL, the value `/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource` publishes as `resource`. Expired, not-yet-valid or wrongly signed tokens are refused. The holder is named `did:web:<domain>:users:<subject>`, where `<domain>` is the knowledge base's `[site] domain` and `<subject>` is the value of the claim `[identity] subjectClaim` selects, which must be a non-empty string, percent-encoded. `email` must be present, and `email_verified: false` is refused. `name` and `picture` are displayed when present. Capabilities ride a flat `roles` array: `semiont-service` lets a service account exchange its token at `POST /api/tokens/agent`, and `semiont-worker` additionally grants the agent token it receives the right to claim jobs.\n\n**Agent tokens** — minted by the gateway at `POST /api/tokens/agent`. Signed HS256 with the gateway's `JWT_SECRET` key ring (the first key signs, every key verifies); `iss` is the knowledge base's domain; they live one hour. Claims: exactly `did` (`did:web:<domain>:agents:<provider>:<model>`, the provider and model each URI-encoded), `email` (`<provider>-<model>@agents.<host>`, every run of characters outside `[A-Za-z0-9-]` in `<provider>:<model>` becoming one `-` and none left at either end, and `<host>` the domain up to its first `:`), `name` (`<provider> <model>`), `domain`, `iss`, `iat`, `exp`, and `roles: [\"semiont-worker\"]` when the minter carried that role.\n\nEither kind names its holder by a `UserId`; a token whose claims do not make one names no principal and is refused. Whatever route it reaches, the verified holder's DID is what `POST /bus/emit` stamps as `_userId`, and the token's `roles` what it stamps as `_roles`. How every name here is formed — the encoding exactly, with cases — is specs/src/principals/cases.json."
      },
      "mediaToken": {
        "type": "apiKey",
        "in": "query",
        "name": "token",
        "description": "A media token from `POST /api/tokens/media`: HS256 under the same key ring as agent tokens, naming one resource (`purpose: \"media\"`, `sub: <resourceId>`) and valid for five minutes. Accepted only by `GET /api/resources/{id}`, and only for the resource it names — for `<img>`, PDF.js and download links, which cannot send a header."
      }
    },
